Write a query using an INNER JOIN to fetch all orders with customer and product details:



SELECT o.order_id, c.name AS customer_name, p.product_name, o.quantity, o.order_date
FROM orders o
INNER JOIN customers c ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id
INNER JOIN products p ON o.product_id = p.product_id;


Use a LEFT JOIN to fetch all customers and their orders, including those without orders:



SELECT c.name AS customer_name, o.order_id, o.quantity
FROM customers c
LEFT JOIN orders o ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id;


Write a query with a FULL OUTER JOIN to combine all customer and product data (simulate if not supported):



SELECT c.name AS customer_name, p.product_name
FROM customers c
FULL OUTER JOIN products p ON c.customer_id = p.product_id;


Use aliases to simplify the query syntax:



SELECT o.order_id, cu.name, pr.product_name
FROM orders o
INNER JOIN customers cu ON o.customer_id = cu.customer_id
INNER JOIN products pr ON o.product_id = pr.product_id;


Write a query to find customers who have never placed an order:



SELECT c.name
FROM customers c
LEFT JOIN orders o ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id
WHERE o.order_id IS NULL;


Write a query using a self-join to identify duplicate orders (if any):



SELECT o1.order_id, o2.order_id
FROM orders o1
INNER JOIN orders o2 ON o1.customer_id = o2.customer_id AND o1.order_id != o2.order_id;


Combine GROUP BY with JOIN to calculate total revenue per customer:



SELECT c.name AS customer_name, SUM(o.quantity * p.price) AS total_revenue
FROM customers c
INNER JOIN orders o ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id
INNER JOIN products p ON o.product_id = p.product_id
GROUP BY c.name;
